---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Corrections Policy"
  description="How MoneyMattersDaily handles errors and updates to published articles."
>
  <h1>Corrections Policy</h1>

  <p>
    Personal finance content is full of numbers, rules, and rates that
    change over time or that we can simply get wrong. Here's how we handle
    it when that happens.
  </p>

  <h2>If you spot an error</h2>
  <p>
    Tell us. The fastest way is our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> —
    include the article and, if you can, what looked wrong and why. We
    read every message that comes in.
  </p>

  <h2>How we fix it</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Factual errors</strong> (a wrong number, an outdated rule
    stated as current, a broken or incorrect source link) are corrected in
    the article directly as soon as we can verify the fix. If the error
    was material — meaning it could have led someone to a wrong decision —
    we update the article's "last updated" date and note what changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Typos and small wording fixes</strong> are corrected without a
    separate note, since they don't affect what the article is telling
    you to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Rates and figures that were correct when published but have
    since changed</strong> (interest rates, contribution limits, and
    similar) aren't treated as "errors" — that's expected drift, which is
    why time-sensitive articles carry a "last updated" date and a note to
    verify current numbers before acting. We do periodically revisit
    high-traffic articles to refresh these where they've gone
    meaningfully stale.
  </p>

  <h2>What we won't do</h2>
  <p>
    We won't quietly edit an article to reverse a substantive claim or
    recommendation without noting that a change was made — see our
    <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> for how we handle
    "last updated" notes on material changes.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
